                                                         <u> AB 1
                                                         </u> Page  1

Date of Hearing:   February 3, 1999

                ASSEMBLY COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION 
                      Kerry Mazzoni, Chair
        AB 1 (Aanestad) - As Introduced: December 7, 1998
 <u> 
SUBJECT </u> :   School Finance

 <u> SUMMARY </u> :   An urgency measure that extends funding for a  
specific school district.  Specifically, <u> this bill </u> : authorizes  
the Plumas Unified School District (PUSD) to be eligible to  
continue to receive "necessary small schools" funding by  
repealing the June 30, 1999 sunset of such funding that exists  
in current law.

 <u> EXISTING LAW </u>  

1)Provides additional funding to "necessary small schools" if  
  they reside in a district with fewer than 2,501 units of ADA.   
  A "necessary small school" is defined as follows: (a)  
  elementary schools with average ADA of less than 101 and that  
  meet other specifications; and (b) high schools with average  
  ADA of less than 301.
2)Pursuant to Chapter 865 of 1997 (SB 1155 Leslie) authorizes  
  the Plumas Unified School District (PUSD) to be eligible to  
  receive "necessary small schools" funding by: (1) specifying  
  narrow criteria that only this district meets; and (2) by  
  increasing the average daily attendance (ADA) cutoff for  
  program eligibility from 2,501 ADA or less to 4,501 ADA.   
  Chapter 865 sunsets June 30, 1999.

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :   Continued funding of approximately $660,000 to  
the PUSD.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> :   

 <u> Arguments in Support </u> .  According to proponents, Plumas Unified  
School District faces unique circumstances which merit its  
inclusion among districts that receive necessary small schools  
funding.  First, Plumas Unified serves an entire county that is  
geographically larger than Rhode Island or Delaware -- a  
situation that leads to higher-than-average operations costs.   
Second, Plumas Unified had experienced a sharp drop in district  
revenues due to a recent downturn in the timber industry and the  
consequent drop in federal Forest Reserve funds.








                                                         <u> AB 1
                                                         </u> Page  2


 <u> Why the Sunset </u> .  The sunset was added to the funding in order to  
reassess the level of potential federal funding the district may  
receive. Since there has been no significant change in this  
situation, the district argues that the sunset should be  
eliminated.

 <u> REGISTERED SUPPORT / OPPOSITION </u> :   (as of 1/29/99)

 <u> Support </u> 

Plumas Unified School District
Regional Council of Rural Counties
School Services of California, Inc.







































